\section{MEMS residual-bias qualification and complete-word constraints}
\label{sec:mems-bias-preconditions}

For every COMPLETE-BRMM statement in this section, physical admission includes
Section~\ref{sec:brmm-physical-wave-generator}, its fixed hard family budgets,
and the same generator potential across successive words. Finite-window
compactness alone is not a replacement for that condition.


The following sensor premises accompany the complete BRMM motion source.
They do not replace its joint translational/rotational realization, certify an
assembled sensor, or assert a positive nonlinear P4 margin.  Throughout this
section $\beta$ denotes a true physical residual, $\hat b_a$ the shipping
estimated residual, and $e_b=b_a^{\rm true}-\hat b_a$ their error in the
shipping temperature-centered coordinates.  These three quantities must not
be interchanged.

For the primary bounded-bias motion target of
Theorem~\ref{thm:brmm-bounded-bias-motion}, BIAS0/1 supplies a same-history
true-bias envelope and retains all corrected bias dynamics. Projection gives
compactness of the estimate, and the triangle bound gives compactness of its
error; neither requires bias convergence. BIAS2 is an optional way to sharpen
the motion-coupling gain, not a prerequisite for the compactness lemma.
Any BIAS2 sector actually used must still be proved on the full relevant
graph. Source/model mismatch remains a forcing channel even at zero sensor
noise, so the weaker theorem allows a nonzero residual motion-error floor.

\subsection{BIAS0: physical decomposition and qualification premise}
\label{sec:bias0}
For the declared assembly, temperature, supply, strain and operating range,
assume the qualified accelerometer model admits
\begin{equation}
 b_a^{\rm tot}(t)=b_0+K_T\Delta T(t)
       +K_\varepsilon\varepsilon_{\rm pcb}(t)+\beta(t)+d_{\rm ng}(t).
 \label{eq:bias0-decomposition}
\end{equation}
Here $d_{\rm ng}$ retains unmodeled offset effects; scale-factor error instead
multiplies specific force and is a separate measurement-model disturbance.
Bosch lists BMI270 zero-g offset, temperature drift and PCB-strain offset
magnitudes of $\pm20\,\mathrm{mg}$, $\pm0.25\,\mathrm{mg/K}$ and
$\pm0.010\,\mathrm{mg}/\mu\mathrm{strain}$, respectively, and sensitivity
temperature drift $0.004\,\%/\mathrm K$, under the specified datasheet
conditions \cite{BoschBMI270DS08}.  These component characteristics are not a
joint three-axis residual envelope or a hard guarantee for an assembled board.

The implemented model subtracts its configured temperature mean; it does not
implement a PCB-strain compensation channel.  Define $d_{\rm det}$ to include
\emph{all} deterministic/non-GM terms left after the calibration actually
performed, including any unremoved $b_0$, temperature error and strain offset.
Then the true coordinate needed by shipping projection is
\begin{equation}
 b_a^{\rm true}=\beta+d_{\rm det}.
 \label{eq:bias0-shipping-truth}
\end{equation}
No additional compensation is presumed to exist in the filter.

An admissible stochastic model for each residual axis is
\begin{equation}
 d\beta_i=-\tau_{b,i}^{-1}\beta_i\,dt
       +\sqrt{2\sigma_{b,i}^2/\tau_{b,i}}\,dW_i.
 \label{eq:bias0-gm}
\end{equation}
First-order GM modeling and Allan/autocorrelation identification are supported
by inertial-sensor studies; model fit and time-constant uncertainty must still
be assessed for the particular sensor \cite{Quinchia2013MEMSErrors,
GarciaCrespillo2020GM}.  The deployed $5000\,\mathrm s$ time constant is a
filter setting, not a universal MEMS physical constant.  The qualification
record must state parameter intervals, initial residual envelope, residual
model mismatch, calibration actually applied, and their evidence.  Until
such a record exists, numerical physical constants remain unassigned.

Qualification should include stationary recordings long enough to resolve the
claimed correlation-time range, repeated turn-ons, temperature ramps and
assembly/unit variation, with uncertainty and fit residuals retained.
Quinchia et al. use seven hours of static data in their study
\cite{Quinchia2013MEMSErrors}; that duration is an example, not a universal
sufficiency rule.  Allan deviation alone does not identify a unique noise law.

\subsection{BIAS1: true-bias history and corrected-error transport}
\begin{theorem}[Conditional homogeneous residual-bias history]
\label{thm:bias1-history}
Under BIAS0, hold the physical GM parameters fixed over a window and set its
stochastic driving and model mismatch to zero for the homogeneous source.
For scalar $\tau_b>0$, its true residual history is
\begin{equation}
 \beta_j=\Phi_b(j,0)\beta_0,\qquad
 \Phi_b(j,\ell)=e^{-(t_j-t_\ell)/\tau_b}I_3.
 \label{eq:bias1-history}
\end{equation}
Thus, conditional on the parameters, the history is the image of one
three-dimensional root cell, with $\|\beta_0\|\le B_b$ if that root envelope
has been qualified.  Per-axis parameters give a diagonal $\Phi_b$; uncertain
parameters remain common source coordinates, not independent event choices.
For nonzero driving, the exact decomposition is
\begin{equation}
 \beta_j=\Phi_b(j,0)\beta_0+
       \sum_{\ell<j}\Phi_b(j,\ell+1)\nu_\ell,
 \label{eq:bias1-forced-history}
\end{equation}
where $\nu_\ell$ is the stochastic convolution over that sample.  In the
stationary scalar-parameter model,
$\operatorname{Cov}(\nu_\ell)=\sigma_b^2(1-e^{-2h_\ell/\tau_b})I_3$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Variation of constants solves Eq.~\eqref{eq:bias0-gm}; iteration of the exact
sample transition gives Eq.~\eqref{eq:bias1-forced-history}.  Integrating the
driving covariance gives the displayed innovation covariance.
\end{proof}

Physical bias continues through estimator hold and H$\to$A release; neither
operation resets the sensor.  For prediction, writing $\hat\phi_j$ for the
shipping factor and $\phi_j$ for the true factor gives exactly
\begin{align}
 e_{b,j+1}^-={}&\hat\phi_j e_{b,j}^+
 +(\phi_j-\hat\phi_j)\beta_j+\nu_j \nonumber\\
 &+d_{{\rm det},j+1}-\hat\phi_jd_{{\rm det},j}.
 \label{eq:bias1-error-prediction}
\end{align}
The matched homogeneous A21 specialization is
$e_{b,j+1}^-=\hat\phi_j e_{b,j}^+$; H18 holds the estimated bias with
$\hat\phi_j=1$ and retains the physical bias as an input.  Parameter mismatch
must remain an explicit disturbance, or be enclosed in a newly verified
homogeneous model; it cannot silently alter the frozen P3 matrices.

At \emph{every} shipping Joseph correction, including S and vector events,
let $y_j$ use the physical error-map sign convention and $K_{b,j}$ be the
actual bias rows of the same-event Kalman gain.  With radius $R_b=0.4$,
\begin{align}
 \hat b_{a,j}^{\rm corr}&=\hat b_{a,j}^-+K_{b,j}y_j,\nonumber\\
 r_{{\rm proj},j}&=\hat b_{a,j}^{\rm corr}
                -\operatorname{Proj}_{R_b}(\hat b_{a,j}^{\rm corr}),\nonumber\\
 e_{b,j}^+&=e_{b,j}^- -K_{b,j}y_j+r_{{\rm proj},j}.
 \label{eq:bias1-error-correction}
\end{align}
Bias coordinates are unchanged by the attitude reset.  Consequently the
\emph{corrected estimation error} is not the free GM history of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:bias1-history}.  Kalman corrections and projection defects are
endogenous graph coordinates, including at zero external noise; they must not
be charged to an exogenous ISS budget.  Removing stochastic driving also does
not remove any process $Q$, covariance floor or gain from the Riccati word.

\begin{lemma}[Projection compatibility with qualified true bias]
\label{lem:bias1-projection}
If $\|b_a^{\rm true}\|\le R_b$ at a correction event, projection preserves
zero error and
\begin{equation}
 \|b_a^{\rm true}-\operatorname{Proj}_{R_b}(b_a^{\rm true}-e_b)\|
 \le\|e_b\|.
 \label{eq:bias1-projection}
\end{equation}
For the matched homogeneous history, a qualified $B_b\le R_b$ suffices.
With mismatch/forcing, the envelope must also cover $d_{\rm det}$ and the
same-history convolution in Eq.~\eqref{eq:bias1-forced-history} at every prefix.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Euclidean ball projection fixes $b_a^{\rm true}$ in the ball and is
nonexpansive.  Apply it to $b_a^{\rm true}$ and $b_a^{\rm true}-e_b$.
\end{proof}
This is a bias-block Euclidean statement, not contraction in the full coupled
$P^{-1}$ metric.  Neither the estimated $0.35$ active-state interior nor the
$0.4$ startup error envelope qualifies $B_b$.  If true bias is outside the
ball, the exact projection offset
$b_a^{\rm true}-\operatorname{Proj}_{R_b}(b_a^{\rm true})$ is nonzero and
must remain an explicit forcing term; zero-error invariance is not available.

\subsection{BIAS2: conditional separation on the full nonlinear graph}
\begin{theorem}[Sufficient complete-word distinguishability condition]
\label{thm:bias2-separation}
Fix the exact full H18/A21 same-history graph, its chart/domain, and every
shipping event.  At accelerometer events set
\[
 g_j=(E_j-I)\hat f_j+E_j\hat R_j\delta a_{w,j},\qquad
 y_j=g_j+e_{b,j}^-.
\]
Stack $a=(R_{a,j}^{-1/2}g_j)_j$ and
$d=(R_{a,j}^{-1/2}e_{b,j}^-)_j$.  Suppose validated constants
$0\le\kappa<1$, $\alpha>0$ satisfy, uniformly on that graph,
\begin{equation}
 2|a^\top d|\le\kappa(\|a\|^2+\|d\|^2),\qquad
 \|a\|^2+\|d\|^2\ge\alpha\,\Xi(z_W),
 \label{eq:bias2-premise}
\end{equation}
where $\Xi(z_W)=z_W^\top Xz_W\ge0$ is the declared quadratic target on the
full word coordinate.  Then
\begin{equation}
 \sum_j\|y_j\|_{R_{a,j}^{-1}}^2
 \ge\mu_{\rm sep}\Xi(z_W),\qquad
 \mu_{\rm sep}=(1-\kappa)\alpha>0.
 \label{eq:bias2-separation}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand $\|a+d\|^2$ and apply both inequalities in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:bias2-premise}.
\end{proof}
BIAS0/1 and an oscillatory sea spectrum do \emph{not} establish
Eq.~\eqref{eq:bias2-premise}.  The signal $g_j$ also depends on evolving
estimation errors, gravity and feedback; it is not the physical marine acceleration.
Even for free bias, $g_j=-\Phi_b(j,0)\beta_0$ shows why a temporal GM
constraint alone cannot imply positive separation.  Whether an analogous
cancellation is admitted by the actual BRMM/error graph is the numerical
obligation, not a new exclusion imposed on the source.

If $y_W=C_yz_W$ with exact nonlinear defects retained in $z_W$, a verified
Eq.~\eqref{eq:bias2-separation} supplies the dense sector
\begin{equation}
 \Pi_{\rm sep}=C_y^\top\operatorname{diag}(R_{a,j}^{-1})C_y
                    -\mu_{\rm sep}X,
 \qquad z_W^\top\Pi_{\rm sep}z_W\ge0.
 \label{eq:bias2-sector}
\end{equation}
It enters Eq.~\eqref{eq:brmm-motion-master} with a nonnegative multiplier.
This does not change the signed Joseph information weight or
discard nonlinear curvature.  All 21 states, cross terms, resets, process
covariances and actual-applied $R_S$ suffix events remain.  No positive
$\mu_{\rm sep}$ is currently certified.  Every prefix uses its own valid
restricted graph and finite-gain master, never an endpoint sector containing
unexecuted future events.
A prefix need not possess positive BIAS2 separation: it may omit that sector
and certify a larger finite gain using its other valid graph constraints.

\subsection{Stationary stochastic corollary}
For a stationary scalar-axis OU residual, its autocorrelation and two-sided
angular-frequency PSD are
\[
 R_\beta(\Delta)=\sigma_b^2e^{-|\Delta|/\tau_b},\qquad
 S_\beta(\omega)=\frac{2\sigma_b^2\tau_b}{1+(\omega\tau_b)^2}.
\]
Integrating over both bands
$[-\omega_2,-\omega_1]\cup[\omega_1,\omega_2]$, with
$0\le\omega_1<\omega_2$, and using
$\sigma_b^2=(2\pi)^{-1}\int_{\mathbb R}S_\beta(\omega)d\omega$ gives
\begin{equation}
 \eta_b=\frac2\pi\left[\arctan(\omega_2\tau_b)
                      -\arctan(\omega_1\tau_b)\right].
 \label{eq:bias-stochastic-band}
\end{equation}
This is a variance fraction, not a finite-window pathwise bound.  Stationarity,
initial transients, parameter uncertainty, sampling/aliasing and the actual
disturbance transfer must be respected in an ISS/moment corollary.  Gaussian
OU forcing has unbounded support and nondifferentiable sample paths; neither a
deterministic amplitude/derivative cap nor a BRMM source restriction follows
from its PSD.  Stochastic bias forcing remains separate from homogeneous P4.
